  "difficulty": "Advanced",
  "introduction": {
    "overview": "A geometric progression (GP) multiplies each term by a fixed common ratio r. This chapter covers the nth term, the sum of n terms, the sum to infinity when |r| < 1, the geometric mean, and word problems such as bouncing balls, doubling populations and repeating decimals.",
    "placementRelevance": "GP questions appear in aptitude tests as number series, compound growth and recurring-decimal problems.",
    "realWorldContext": "Compound interest, population growth and the decay of a bouncing ball follow geometric patterns."
  },
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Use Tₙ = a rⁿ⁻¹",
    "Sum n terms for r ≠ 1",
    "Sum to infinity for |r| < 1",
    "Use three-term GP tricks",
    "Convert recurring decimals into fractions"
  ],
  "prerequisiteKnowledge": {
    "requiredTopics": [
      "Sequences and Series",
      "Arithmetic Progressions"
    ],
    "quickRefresher": "In a GP the ratio of consecutive terms is constant. Terms equally spaced from the ends have the same product."
  },
  "conceptExplanation": {
    "coreDefinitions": [
      "First term a and common ratio r.",
      "nth term: Tₙ = a rⁿ⁻¹.",
      "Sum of n terms: Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) for r ≠ 1.",
      "Sum to infinity: S∞ = a/(1 − r), valid only when |r| < 1.",
      "Geometric mean of x and y: √(xy)."
    ],
    "theoreticalIntuition": "Each step multiplies, so growth or decay is exponential. When |r| < 1 the terms shrink so fast that the infinite sum is finite.",
    "visualOrMentalModel": "Find r by dividing consecutive terms. Use the sum formula with the ratio greater than 1 on top (rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) when r > 1."
  },
  "importantRulesAndFormulas": [
    {
      "title": "nth term",
      "formula": "Tₙ = a rⁿ⁻¹",
      "description": "Any GP.",
      "whenToUse": "Find a term.",
      "simpleExample": "a = 3, r = 2: T₆ = 96"
    },
    {
      "title": "Finite sum",
      "formula": "Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1)",
      "description": "r ≠ 1.",
      "whenToUse": "Sum of n terms.",
      "simpleExample": "3 + 6 + 12 + 24 = 45"
    },
    {
      "title": "Infinite sum",
      "formula": "S∞ = a/(1 − r)",
      "description": "|r| < 1.",
      "whenToUse": "Shrinking GP.",
      "simpleExample": "8 + 4 + 2 + … = 16"
    },
    {
      "title": "Three in GP",
      "formula": "a/r, a, ar",
      "description": "Product known.",
      "whenToUse": "Middle term = cube root of product.",
      "simpleExample": "Product 216: middle 6"
    },
    {
      "title": "Geometric mean",
      "formula": "GM = √(xy)",
      "description": "Two numbers.",
      "whenToUse": "Insert a middle term.",
      "simpleExample": "GM of 4 and 9 is 6"
    }
  ],
  "detailedExplanation": {
    "mainNarrative": [
      "In 3, 6, 12, 24 the ratio is 2. The 6th term is 3 × 2⁵ = 96, and the sum of 4 terms is 3(2⁴ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 45.",
      "For a shrinking GP, 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + … has r = 1/2, so S∞ = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 16.",
      "A recurring decimal is an infinite GP. 0.363636… = 0.36 + 0.0036 + … has a = 0.36 and r = 0.01, so the sum is 0.36/0.99 = 4/11.",
      "For three numbers in GP use a/r, a, ar. Their product is a³, so a is the cube root of the product. If the product is 216 then a = 6.",
      "A ball dropped from 100 m that rebounds to 3/5 of its previous height travels 100 + 2 × (60 + 36 + …) = 100 + 2 × 60/(1 − 3/5) = 100 + 300 = 400 m in total."
    ],
    "deepDiveAspects": [
      {
        "title": "Sum of n terms for r < 1",
        "content": "Use a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) to avoid negative signs."
      },
      {
        "title": "Terms and product",
        "content": "The product of the terms equidistant from the ends is constant, which gives the product of all terms as (a·l)ⁿ⁄²."
      }
    ]
  },
